[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Document ID Generation & Auto-IDs vs Custom IDs in Firebase Firestore"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Choosing the right strategy for document identification is one of the most critical architectural decisions when designing a Firebase Firestore database. Document IDs dictate how data is indexed, how security rules are written, and how efficiently you can query your data."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Firestore Document IDs"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Every document in Firestore is uniquely identified by its path and its document ID. Unlike relational databases that often rely on auto-incrementing integers, Firestore uses strings for all document IDs. When you create a document, you can either let Firestore generate a random ID for you or specify a custom, meaningful ID."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Option 1: Auto-Generated IDs (Firestore Default)"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"When you use the add() method in the Firebase SDK, Firestore automatically generates a unique 20-character string using a cryptographically secure pseudo-random number generator. These IDs are highly randomized and uniformly distributed."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: Extremely safe from collision even at high write volumes. Prevents hot-spotting on specific database server nodes."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: Completely non-descriptive. You cannot infer any information about the document from the ID alone."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"import { collection, addDoc } from "firebase/firestore";

async function createUser(db, userData) {
  const docRef = await addDoc(collection(db, "users"), userData);
  console.log("Document written with ID: ", docRef.id);
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Option 2: Custom IDs"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Custom IDs involve defining your own string identifiers when creating a document, such as using setDoc() with a specific ID like a user's email, a UUID, or a natural business key like a SKU."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: Makes referencing documents easier without needing to query first. Useful for one-to-one relationships or deterministic lookups."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: Risk of collisions if not managed carefully. Sequential custom IDs (like timestamps or incremental numbers) can cause severe write bottlenecks."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"import { doc, setDoc } from "firebase/firestore";

async function createUserWithCustomId(db, userId, userData) {
  await setDoc(doc(db, "users", userId), userData);
  console.log("User created with custom ID");
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Performance Implications: Hotspotting"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Firestore automatically splits and distributes your data across multiple storage servers based on document paths and IDs to handle high read/write loads. If you use sequential custom IDs (such as date-prefixed strings like 2023-10-01-001, 2023-10-01-002), all your writes will target the exact same database server node, creating a performance bottleneck known as hotspotting."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for ID Selection"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use auto-generated IDs for general logs, messages, and collections where documents do not require natural business keys."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use custom IDs when mapping external systems (like Stripe customer IDs or Auth0 user IDs) directly to Firestore documents."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"If you must use sequential or predictable custom IDs, hash them or prepend a randomized string segment to distribute the write load evenly."}]
